    title: "Reading Passage",
    level: "B1",
    text: "The Art Studio: Where Creativity Meets Discipline\n\nMany people imagine that working in an art studio is a romantic, carefree experience — an artist standing before a canvas, waiting for inspiration to strike. In reality, the life of a professional artist requires as much discipline and organisation as any office job, and the studio itself is a carefully designed workspace where creativity is nurtured through routine.\n\nA well-equipped art studio typically contains a range of materials and tools. Painters need easels, canvases, palettes, brushes of various sizes, and tubes of oil, acrylic, or watercolour paint. Sculptors work with clay, plaster, wood, or metal and use chisels, mallets, and — increasingly — power tools. Printmakers operate heavy presses and use sharp cutting tools to carve designs into blocks of wood or linoleum.\n\nLighting is one of the most important aspects of any studio. Most artists prefer natural light from north-facing windows because it is consistent throughout the day — it doesn't cast strong shadows or change colour as the sun moves. If natural light isn't available, artists use daylight-balanced LED lamps that mimic the qualities of sunlight without the heat.\n\nStudio spaces have changed significantly over the past century. In the early 1900s, artists worked in cold, draughty attics or warehouses because those spaces were cheap and had large windows. Since the mid-20th century, purpose-built studios and shared art spaces have become more common. These co-working studios — where several artists rent individual areas within a larger building — have grown particularly popular since the 2010s. They offer not only affordable workspace but also a sense of community that can prevent the isolation many artists experience.\n\nOne of the biggest challenges artists face is the cost of materials. Professional-quality oil paints, for example, can be extremely expensive, especially those made from rare pigments. Many art schools have started teaching students about sustainable practices — making their own paints, recycling canvases, and using found objects in their work.",
